  </div>

  <!-- ========== 3. 24-48H DELTA (descriptive, verb-disciplined) ========== -->
  <div class="section" id="delta">
    <h2 class="section-title">
      What Moved (Last 24-48h)
      <span class="meta">Descriptive · banned-verb lint enforced · each paragraph cites a forecast or is marked context-only</span>
    </h2>
    <div class="delta-narrative">
      <p>The two weeks since Day 188 opened with a sharp burst of kinetic exchange. On September 1–2, the United States struck Iranian military targets after approximately one month without a major exchange, and Iran retaliated with missiles and drones aimed at Ahmed al-Jaber Air Base in Kuwait and Al Minhad Air Base in the UAE. US CENTCOM destroyed five Iranian oil tankers by September 9. On the night of September 8–9, Iran launched 20 ballistic missiles at Muwaffaq Salti Air Base in Jordan — and that proved to be the last confirmed major ballistic launch before an undeclared lull took hold. From September 9 through Day 203 (September 18), nine consecutive days elapsed without an Iranian ballistic missile or drone attack on any host state, the longest unannounced gap since the June MOU became defunct. No Iranian authority announced the pause, and no mediator confirmed it. → <span class="context-only">context only</span></p>

      <p>The primary diplomatic mechanism for Hormuz — an Oman-hosted regional meeting with Iran and Gulf Arab states — collapsed before convening. On September 11, Iran announced Oman would host the session on September 14–15 in Salalah to formalize a temporary shipping lane. Iran and Oman had already agreed bilaterally. On September 13 — the day before the meeting — Oman announced an indefinite postponement; subsequent reporting named Saudi Arabia as the party that submitted amendments, arguing the draft wording would establish an unacceptable new status quo endorsing Iranian authority over the waterway. Hormuz daily traffic, which reached a single-day low of seven vessels on September 9, remained in a 6–13 vessel range through September 18 against a pre-war baseline of roughly 85. → moved <span class="cite">F4</span></p>

      <p>Both F2 and F3 elapsed without meeting their YES criteria. In the September 3–6 window relevant to F2, Iranian President Pezeshkian had said on September 1 that Tehran would return to the ceasefire framework "if the US does" — a conditional statement predating the window that lacked a commitment to mediated negotiations. The US publicly ruled out talks on September 4 until Iranian ship attacks stopped; combat continued through September 9. FM Araghchi maintained throughout the period that Iran would not accept a temporary ceasefire and demanded only a complete and lasting end to the war. For F3, the last formally convened indirect negotiating session with a named mediator remained the Islamabad Talks of April 11–12. On September 16–17, President Trump claimed that Iran had made "direct contact" seeking a deal; Iran's SNSC Secretary Rezaei stated on September 15 that no negotiations would proceed until Iranian preconditions were met, and no named Iranian official confirmed any contact. → moved <span class="cite">F2</span>, <span class="cite">F3</span></p>

      <p>Polling across the September 3–19 period tracked a downward shift for Netanyahu's Likud in the individual-party contest. A Channel 13/Haaretz poll published September 17 put Yashar (Eisenkot) at 22 seats and Likud at 18, the latter's lowest recorded figure in the campaign. Across five polls in the cycle, four placed Yashar as the largest single party. At the coalition-bloc level, the same September 17 Channel 13 survey gave the Netanyahu-aligned bloc 54 seats against 52 for the Eisenkot-led opposition — the first major-broadcaster poll to give the Netanyahu bloc an edge rather than a tie. The divergence between individual-party results (Yashar ahead) and bloc-level results (Netanyahu bloc edging forward) is explained by party-count arithmetic: three or four small Netanyahu-adjacent parties are polling above threshold while the Eisenkot bloc's smaller allies are closer to the 3.25% floor. → moved <span class="cite">F5</span></p>

      <p>Iranian FM Araghchi traveled to Beijing on September 17–18, telling Chinese FM Wang Yi that Iran and Oman had finalized a Hormuz reopening plan but that the US must first fulfill its ceasefire obligations — a framing Oman did not publicly confirm. Araghchi also declared readiness to deepen strategic coordination with China. Trump's "direct contact" claim — made as foreign ministers gathered for UN General Assembly opening week in New York — drew no substantive Iranian response through Day 203. Araghchi arrived at UNGA as Iran's lead voice; his public posture framed the Hormuz file as a regional matter and the bilateral dispute as the US's problem to resolve first. UNGA sidelines (September 22–26) remain the next structural opportunity for any form of contact. → <span class="context-only">context only</span></p>
    </div>
  </div>

  <!-- ========== 4. SCENARIO MAP (computed summary) ========== -->
  <div class="section" id="scenario-map">
    <h2 class="section-title">
      Scenario Map
      <span class="meta">Computed from Forecast Board · 1 sentence per scenario</span>
    </h2>
    <div class="scenario-map">
      <div class="prob-bar-container">
        <div class="prob-bar prob-escalation" style="width:40%;">Escalation 40%</div>
        <div class="prob-bar prob-status" style="width:48%;">Protracted 48%</div>
        <div class="prob-bar prob-deescalation" style="width:12%;">De-esc 12%</div>
      </div>
      <p class="scenario-line"><strong class="label-esc">Escalation path (40%):</strong> No ceasefire active, fire pause undeclared and without sponsor, Iranian preconditions unmet, Salalah shipping mechanism collapsed — the nine-day lull has no confirmed institutional support and could end without warning.</p>
      <p class="scenario-line"><strong class="label-prot">Protracted path (48%):</strong> Fire pause persists without diplomatic infrastructure to convert it; Hormuz frozen near 10 transits/day; Israeli election consumes October attention on both Washington and Jerusalem tracks; no party has an incentive to move before October 27 results are known.</p>
      <p class="scenario-line"><strong class="label-desc">De-escalation path (12%):</strong> Requires N1 (Iran confirms US contact at UNGA, p=0.15), N2 (Gulf meeting convened, p=0.22), and F4 (Hormuz traffic responding, p=0.05) to move in concert — a sequence with no active mechanism at any step as of Day 204.</p>
    </div>
  </div>

  <!-- ========== 5. INSIDE IRAN (descriptive, ~200 words) ========== -->
  <div class="section" id="inside-iran">
    <h2 class="section-title">
      Inside Iran
      <span class="meta">State-media decode · 200 words · verb-ban + forecast-cite rule applies</span>
    </h2>
    <div class="inside-iran">
      <div class="iran-media-badges">
        <span class="iran-media-badge">IRNA</span>
        <span class="iran-media-badge">IRIB</span>
        <span class="iran-media-badge">Press TV</span>
        <span class="iran-media-badge">Fars</span>
        <span class="iran-media-badge">Tasnim</span>
        <span class="iran-media-badge">Kayhan</span>
      </div>
      <p>Iran's state media — IRNA, Tasnim, IRIB, and Press TV — framed the September 3–19 period primarily as a story of American aggression met with Iranian principled restraint. The September 1–2 resumption of US strikes was covered as an unprovoked escalation that obligated Iranian defensive responses. Presidential remarks on September 1, expressing Tehran's readiness to return to ceasefire conditions if Washington did the same, received heavy IRNA amplification as evidence of Iranian good faith; state media did not present this as a concession or as an admission that Iran sought negotiations. → <span class="context-only">context only</span></p>

      <p>The informal fire pause that began around September 9 received no official acknowledgment. IRIB and Tasnim neither announced it nor attributed it to any Iranian decision; it appeared in coverage only as an absence of continued US strike activity, never as an Iranian operational choice. The planned Salalah meeting received substantial pre-September 13 coverage as proof that Iran was resolving Hormuz through regional consensus rather than American mediation; its postponement on September 13 was attributed exclusively to Saudi Arabia acting under US pressure. Iran's foreign ministry spokesman presented Tehran as the cooperative party that had agreed to a Muscat-backed plan. No critical coverage of Iran's own role in the postponement appeared in state media. → <span class="context-only">context only</span></p>

      <p>Araghchi's Beijing visit was the highest-profile state-media story of the period. IRNA and Press TV framed the China-Iran strategic declaration as evidence of a deepening alternative to Western-dominated diplomacy. Trump's September 16–17 "direct contact" claim drew only brief dismissal from Tasnim, which called it a fabrication prepared for domestic consumption during UNGA week; no other state outlet engaged it substantively. SNSC Secretary Rezaei's September 15 statement rejecting US negotiations until preconditions were met received significant IRIB airtime, framed as a principled sovereignty position. The net state-media narrative: Iran is willing, the US is not; the strait is a regional file; China is a reliable partner. → <span class="context-only">context only</span></p>
    </div>
  </div>

